1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,960
Okay, so you want to buy a shopping

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:06,000
mall, go behind a big empty building,

3
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:08,320
fill it with stores, and watch the rent

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,120
checks roll in every month. Hundreds of

5
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:11,960
shoppers walking past your stores every

6
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:13,840
single day, and every one of those

7
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:16,160
stores pays you just for the privilege

8
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:18,320
of standing there. Sounds simple. It is

9
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,800
not simple. And the story of how malls

10
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:21,880
actually make money, and how they

11
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,920
actually die, is one of the strangest

12
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:25,520
stories in all of real estate. Before

13
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:27,320
you make a single dollar, you need to

14
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:29,600
survive buying the thing, or building

15
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:32,040
it. A large regional mall is not a small

16
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,920
purchase. Building one from scratch on

17
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,760
empty land with all the parking, the

18
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,040
plumbing, the electrical systems, the

19
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,840
climate control, and the permits can run

20
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:42,080
into the hundreds of millions of dollars

21
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,680
before a single store opens its doors.

22
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:45,680
The land alone can cost tens of

23
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:47,720
millions. Then you pour the concrete,

24
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:49,600
put up the steel, run miles of wiring

25
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:51,600
and ductwork, build out multiple levels

26
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:54,080
of parking, and satisfy a small mountain

27
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,000
of local zoning and safety regulations.

28
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,840
By the time the ribbon gets cut, you can

29
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,040
easily have spent more than the price of

30
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:02,200
a small hospital. Buying an existing

31
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:04,000
mall is usually cheaper up front, but

32
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:06,320
that's where things get interesting,

33
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,160
because not all malls are the same mall.

34
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:09,560
There is a huge gap in this industry

35
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,960
between what people in the business call

36
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:13,920
class A malls and everything below them.

37
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:16,080
A class A mall is the kind with a luxury

38
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:18,440
department store, high sales per square

39
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:20,400
foot, a waiting list of brands wanting

40
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:23,000
to get in, and a location near wealthy

41
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,000
suburbs. Those trade for enormous sums,

42
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,680
sometimes north of a billion dollars for

43
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,880
the best ones, because the income they

44
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:30,400
produce is stable and predictable. Then

45
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:32,760
there are the malls two or three tiers

46
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,560
down. Older buildings, weaker anchor

47
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:36,600
stores, lower income neighborhoods

48
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:38,280
nearby. Some of these have sold for a

49
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:40,520
tiny fraction of what it cost to build

50
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:44,120
them. One mall in Akron, Ohio, called

51
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:46,600
Rolling Acres, sold for $2.75 million in

52
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:48,480
the early 2000s, and that was considered

53
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,960
a lot less than what the previous owner

54
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:54,760
had paid, which was over $33 million.

55
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:57,000
A mall that once had over 140 stores

56
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:58,720
eventually sold again for well under $2

57
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,760
million dollars and it kept losing

58
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:02,320
tenants after that. So, here is the

59
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:04,600
first thing nobody tells you when they

60
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:06,640
picture owning a mall, you're not really

61
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:08,479
buying a building, you're buying a bet

62
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,399
on a neighborhood and the price tag

63
00:02:08,479 --> 00:02:12,640
tells you almost everything about which

64
00:02:10,399 --> 00:02:14,840
bet you're making. Most people who own

65
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:17,240
malls today by the way don't own them

66
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:18,840
the way you'd own a house. They own them

67
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:21,560
through a structure called a real estate

68
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:22,840
investment trust or REIT for short. A

69
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:25,160
REIT is a company that owns

70
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:26,840
income-producing property and is legally

71
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:29,040
required to pay out almost all of its

72
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:30,640
profit to shareholders every year in

73
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,680
exchange for not having to pay corporate

74
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:34,959
income tax on that money. This structure

75
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:37,040
is a huge part of why large-scale mall

76
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:38,920
ownership exists in its current form.

77
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:41,040
Ordinary investors can buy shares in a

78
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:43,000
mall owning REIT on the stock market for

79
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:44,560
the price of a cup of coffee without

80
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,360
ever having to personally deal with a

81
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:48,520
leaking roof or a collapsed anchor

82
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:50,680
tenant. The largest of these companies

83
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:52,520
own dozens, sometimes over a hundred,

84
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:55,040
shopping centers at once spreading the

85
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:57,000
risk of any single mall failing across a

86
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:58,720
much bigger portfolio. That's a very

87
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,800
different game than the one facing a

88
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:03,000
private buyer who puts everything into

89
00:03:00,800 --> 00:03:04,920
one building in one town. Say you've

90
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,160
settled on a mall and the purchase price

91
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:09,000
seems reasonable. Before you collect a

92
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:11,239
single rent check, there's a layer of

93
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,239
costs that catches almost every new mall

94
00:03:11,239 --> 00:03:15,280
owner off guard. First, there are the

95
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:17,080
anchor leases you inherit and you don't

96
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,720
get to renegotiate most of them right

97
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:21,680
away. Anchor stores, the big department

98
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:23,880
stores that sit at the ends of the mall,

99
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:26,040
often sign their leases decades ago,

100
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:28,120
sometimes for shockingly low rent and

101
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:29,519
those leases can run for many more years

102
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:31,480
before you get a chance to raise the

103
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:34,239
price. You inherit whatever deal the

104
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:35,720
last owner made, good or bad. Second,

105
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:37,800
there's deferred maintenance. Roofs

106
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:39,720
leak, HVAC systems that cool and heat

107
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:42,040
over a million square feet of space wear

108
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:44,080
out, parking lots crack, escalators and

109
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:45,760
elevators need constant service. If the

110
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:47,680
previous owner was cutting corners to

111
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:49,560
keep cash flow looking good on paper,

112
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:51,560
you find out about it the first winter

113
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:53,760
you own the place, usually in the form

114
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:55,280
of a very large repair bill. Third,

115
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:57,520
there are what's called co-tenancy

116
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:59,440
clauses buried in almost every mall

117
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:02,120
lease. These are contract terms that

118
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:04,120
say, in effect, if a major anchor store

119
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:06,200
closes, the smaller stores around it are

120
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:07,520
allowed to pay reduced rent, or in some

121
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:09,640
cases walk away from their lease

122
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:11,640
entirely. You don't see this cost on the

123
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:13,440
day you buy the mall. You see it the day

124
00:04:11,640 --> 00:04:15,040
your anchor tenant leaves, and suddenly

125
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:17,160
a dozen other tenants are legally

126
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:18,680
allowed to pay you less, or nothing at

127
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:21,079
all. That's the trapdoor under the

128
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:22,880
floor. The mall looks fully rented on

129
00:04:21,079 --> 00:04:25,080
the day you sign the papers. But, the

130
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:26,960
moment one big piece falls out, the

131
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:29,040
whole financial structure underneath it

132
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:30,960
can shift without you doing anything at

133
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:32,840
all. Here's where it gets interesting,

134
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:35,080
because the way a mall makes money is

135
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:37,360
more layered than most people realize.

136
00:04:35,080 --> 00:04:39,440
The first and simplest layer is base

137
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:41,640
rent. Every store pays a fixed monthly

138
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,080
amount for its space, usually priced per

139
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:45,919
square foot per year. A small specialty

140
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:48,200
store might pay somewhere around $40 to

141
00:04:45,919 --> 00:04:50,360
$60 per square foot per year in a strong

142
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:53,000
mall. A department store anchor, by

143
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:55,120
contrast, often pays a tiny fraction of

144
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:57,000
that. In fact, according to real estate

145
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:59,120
analysts who studied Sears store leases

146
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,600
before the company's bankruptcy, Sears

147
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:03,600
was often paying somewhere around $5 per

148
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:05,760
square foot in rent, while the average

149
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:08,120
rent at top quality malls was closer to

150
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:10,919
$50 or $60 per square foot for other

151
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:12,560
tenants. Read that again. The store with

152
00:05:10,919 --> 00:05:14,720
the biggest sign and the most floor

153
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:16,320
space was paying roughly 1/10 what the

154
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:19,000
little clothing shop next door was

155
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:21,080
paying per square foot. Why would a mall

156
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,720
owner accept that? Because the anchor

157
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:24,480
isn't really paying for space. It's

158
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:26,560
paying for its name. The anchor brings

159
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:28,800
in customers. Those customers walk past

160
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,960
dozens of smaller stores on their way in

161
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:33,000
and out. The mall owner isn't renting

162
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:34,800
square footage to the anchor. The mall

163
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,560
owner is renting foot traffic from the

164
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,800
anchor, and paying for it with a rent

165
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,680
discount.

166
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:41,480
Every other store in the building is, in

167
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:42,760
a sense, subsidizing that discount,

168
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:44,680
because they're the ones paying full

169
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:46,919
price to be near it. The second layer of

170
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:49,200
income is called percentage rent. This

171
00:05:46,919 --> 00:05:50,920
is money on top of the base rent tied

172
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:53,200
directly to how much a store actually

173
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:55,280
sells. A typical structure works like

174
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:57,400
this. A store agrees to pay its base

175
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:59,720
rent, but if its yearly sales cross a

176
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:01,560
certain amount, called the breakpoint,

177
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:03,920
the mall takes a small percentage of

178
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:05,880
everything sold above that line. So, if

179
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:07,760
a store's breakpoint is set at 1 and 1/2

180
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:09,800
million dollars a year, and the store

181
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:12,600
sells 2 million dollars that year, the

182
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:15,000
mall might take somewhere around 6 or 7%

183
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:16,320
of that extra $500,000.

184
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,120
That's real money that scales

185
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:20,520
automatically with how well the mall

186
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:22,760
itself is performing without the mall

187
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:25,080
owner lifting a finger. The third layer

188
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:27,360
is called common area maintenance, or

189
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:29,080
CAM for short. This is separate from

190
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:30,560
rent. It's the tenants paying their

191
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:32,680
share of the cost of running the

192
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:34,360
building itself, cleaning the floors,

193
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:36,480
lighting the hallways, maintaining the

194
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:38,680
parking lot, running security, keeping

195
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:40,600
the escalators moving. Each tenant pays

196
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:43,080
a portion based on how much space they

197
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:45,040
occupy compared to the whole mall. If a

198
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:47,000
mall's shared cost for the year come to

199
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:49,280
$250,000,

200
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,560
and a tenant occupies 10% of the mall's

201
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:54,520
total leasable space, that tenant owes

202
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:56,400
$25,000 of that bill for the year. This

203
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:58,280
is important because it means a big

204
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:00,280
chunk of what it costs to physically run

205
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:02,400
the building isn't paid by the mall

206
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:04,040
owner directly, it's billed straight

207
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:05,880
back to the tenants. Then there's a

208
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:07,520
fourth category that most people never

209
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,800
think about at all. Everything that

210
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:11,919
isn't a store, parking fees in dense

211
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:14,040
urban malls, advertising space on

212
00:07:11,919 --> 00:07:16,000
digital screens and directory boards,

213
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:17,919
kiosks in the middle of the walkway,

214
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,520
which often charge extremely high rent

215
00:07:17,919 --> 00:07:21,560
per square foot because they take up

216
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:23,919
almost no space and sit exactly where

217
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:26,520
foot traffic is heaviest. Pop-up events,

218
00:07:23,919 --> 00:07:28,120
holiday markets, seasonal photo booths.

219
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:30,320
None of these show up in most people's

220
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:31,760
mental picture of owning a mall, but for

221
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:33,760
some properties they add up to a

222
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:35,760
meaningful slice of total income.

223
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:37,680
There's a counterintuitive detail hiding

224
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:39,640
inside that kiosk math worth pointing

225
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,840
out. A kiosk in the middle of a busy

226
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:44,320
walkway might occupy less than 100

227
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:46,120
square feet of floor space. And yet,

228
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:48,200
because it sits directly in the path of

229
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:50,160
every single shopper passing through, it

230
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:52,240
can be priced at a far higher rate per

231
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:54,520
square foot than a full-size store along

232
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:56,720
the wall. Size and rent, in other words,

233
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:58,840
are not the same thing in this business.

234
00:07:56,720 --> 00:08:00,640
Position is worth more than space. A

235
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:02,720
tiny cart selling sunglasses in the

236
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:04,440
busiest intersection of the mall can,

237
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:06,320
per square foot, be one of the most

238
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:09,040
valuable pieces of real estate in the

239
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:10,680
entire building. So far, the mall sounds

240
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:12,640
like a machine that prints money from

241
00:08:10,680 --> 00:08:14,040
four different directions at once. Now,

242
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:16,040
here's the other side of the ledger.

243
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:18,360
Utilities on a building of this size are

244
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:20,560
enormous. Keeping over a million square

245
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:22,120
feet climate controlled year-round, lit,

246
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:24,760
and running is one of the largest

247
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:27,000
recurring expenses a mall owner faces.

248
00:08:24,760 --> 00:08:29,000
Add to that security staffing, ongoing

249
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,080
cleaning contracts, landscaping, snow

250
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,320
removal in colder regions, and constant

251
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:35,320
small repairs. A broken sprinkler here,

252
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:37,719
a cracked tile there, and the monthly

253
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:39,120
operating bill for a large mall can run

254
00:08:37,719 --> 00:08:40,800
into the hundreds of thousands of

255
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:42,680
dollars before a single dollar goes

256
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:45,120
toward paying down the loan used to buy

257
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:46,960
the place. Then there's property tax,

258
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:48,920
which on a large commercial property in

259
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:51,400
a major metro area can be one of the

260
00:08:48,920 --> 00:08:53,320
single largest line items of the year.

261
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:55,160
And insurance, which has been climbing

262
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:57,040
sharply across the entire commercial

263
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:59,240
real estate industry in recent years,

264
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:01,280
especially for older buildings. There's

265
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:03,000
also a detail buried inside the CAM

266
00:09:01,280 --> 00:09:05,760
structure that catches new owners off

267
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,080
guard. Those shared costs don't shrink

268
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:08,880
just because some of the mall sits

269
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:10,800
empty. The hallways still need to be

270
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:12,960
cleaned whether every store beside them

271
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:14,920
is occupied or not. The lights above a

272
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:16,839
vacant storefront still need power.

273
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:19,360
Security still has to patrol the entire

274
00:09:16,839 --> 00:09:21,280
building, not just the leased sections.

275
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:23,200
So, when vacancy rises, the same fixed

276
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:25,280
pool of shared expenses gets divided

277
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:27,280
among fewer paying tenants, which means

278
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:28,920
each remaining tenant's CAM bill goes

279
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:31,080
up, even though nothing about the

280
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:33,120
building's actual upkeep has changed.

281
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:35,120
Rising vacancy doesn't just mean lost

282
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:37,000
rent from the empty stores. It quietly

283
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:39,360
raises the cost of staying in business

284
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,880
for every store that's still open, which

285
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:43,360
is exactly the kind of pressure that

286
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:45,120
pushes marginal tenants to leave, too.

287
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:46,960
And here's the trap almost nobody sees

288
00:09:45,120 --> 00:09:48,920
coming until they own the place. Even a

289
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:50,680
mostly full mall can lose money if the

290
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:52,600
mix is wrong. This is the

291
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:54,440
counterintuitive part. It's not just

292
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:56,320
about how many stores are open, it's

293
00:09:54,440 --> 00:09:58,800
about who's paying real rent and who

294
00:09:56,320 --> 00:10:01,000
isn't. A mall might look at 90% occupied

295
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:03,080
and feel healthy from the outside, but

296
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,200
if half of that occupied space is anchor

297
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:07,040
stores paying next to nothing, and a

298
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:09,080
growing share of the inline stores are

299
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:10,920
on short-term leases at discounted rates

300
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:12,800
just to keep the lights on, the actual

301
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:15,400
cash flow can be far weaker than the

302
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:17,200
occupancy number suggests. Occupancy is

303
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:18,880
not the same thing as income. That

304
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:21,160
distinction is where a lot of mall

305
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:23,400
owners get into serious trouble. Before

306
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:24,960
we get to how malls die, it's worth

307
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:27,000
understanding something that mall

308
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:29,120
developers figured out decades ago,

309
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,040
because it explains almost everything

310
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:32,880
about how these buildings are shaped.

311
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:35,040
The man most credited with designing the

312
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:37,280
modern American mall was a Michigan

313
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:39,160
developer named Alfred Taubman. He

314
00:10:37,280 --> 00:10:41,360
didn't just build shopping centers, he

315
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:43,080
studied in detail how people move

316
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:45,320
through a building and then designed the

317
00:10:43,080 --> 00:10:46,920
building around that movement. He spaced

318
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:48,839
anchor stores apart from each other by

319
00:10:46,920 --> 00:10:51,000
roughly the distance of about three city

320
00:10:48,839 --> 00:10:52,839
blocks, so that shoppers walking from

321
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,720
one big store to the other would pass

322
00:10:52,839 --> 00:10:57,720
dozens of smaller shops along the way.

323
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:59,600
He put escalators and elevators at the

324
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:01,320
ends of hallways instead of the middle,

325
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:03,560
so people would walk the full length of

326
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:05,240
a corridor to reach them. He designed

327
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:07,480
skylights with artificial lighting mixed

328
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:09,640
in, so shoppers couldn't easily tell how

329
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:12,160
much daylight had passed and lose track

330
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:14,000
of time. None of this was decoration.

331
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:15,560
Every choice was designed to maximize

332
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,280
the amount of storefront a shopper's

333
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:19,440
eyes would pass across before they

334
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:21,280
reached what they actually came for.

335
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:24,120
That's the entire commercial logic of a

336
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:26,360
mall in one sentence. Force movement and

337
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:27,960
you increase exposure, and exposure is

338
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:30,640
what the smaller tenants are actually

339
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:32,520
paying for when they sign a lease. Now

340
00:11:30,640 --> 00:11:34,480
here's the structural risk that runs

341
00:11:32,520 --> 00:11:36,400
through this entire business, and it's

342
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:38,280
best explained through something that

343
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:40,240
actually happened across the American

344
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:42,120
mall industry. For most of the 20th

345
00:11:40,240 --> 00:11:44,040
century, the department stores like

346
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:46,240
Sears were the backbone of the shopping

347
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:48,160
mall. They were the reason malls got

348
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:50,600
built in the first place. Developers

349
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:52,040
would often not even begin construction

350
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:54,400
until they had one or two major

351
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:55,920
department stores signed up as anchors

352
00:11:54,400 --> 00:11:57,480
because banks wouldn't lend money for

353
00:11:55,920 --> 00:12:00,040
the project without that guarantee of

354
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:02,760
foot traffic. But starting in the 2000s

355
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:04,480
and accelerating sharply after 2010,

356
00:12:02,760 --> 00:12:07,520
those same department stores began

357
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:09,000
closing at a rapid pace. Sears, once the

358
00:12:07,520 --> 00:12:11,480
largest retailer in the country with

359
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,360
about 4,000 stores at its peak, filed

360
00:12:11,480 --> 00:12:14,880
for bankruptcy protection in October of

361
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:16,720
2018.

362
00:12:14,880 --> 00:12:19,320
At the time, one major mall owner had

363
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:21,480
Sears stores in 59 of its properties.

364
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:24,240
Another had them in 40. Across the

365
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:25,800
industry, roughly 80% of Sears branded

366
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:27,920
stores were located inside regional

367
00:12:25,800 --> 00:12:30,080
malls. Here is why this mattered so much

368
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:32,440
more than closing any ordinary store.

369
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:34,800
Remember the co-tenancy clauses? When an

370
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:36,800
anchor closes, smaller tenants around it

371
00:12:34,800 --> 00:12:39,120
can legally reduce their rent or exit

372
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:41,240
their leases. So one closure doesn't

373
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:43,000
just remove one tenant's rent check. It

374
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:44,920
can trigger a wave of rent reductions

375
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,200
throughout the mall, sometimes affecting

376
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:49,280
dozens of stores at once, all because of

377
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:50,760
contract language written years earlier

378
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:52,400
back when everyone assumed the anchor

379
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:54,200
would stay forever.

380
00:12:52,400 --> 00:12:56,280
And filling that empty anchor space back

381
00:12:54,200 --> 00:12:57,960
up is not simple or fast. These

382
00:12:56,280 --> 00:13:00,640
buildings were built for one specific

383
00:12:57,960 --> 00:13:03,160
kind of tenant, enormous floor plates,

384
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:04,880
sometimes over 100,000 square feet, with

385
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:07,200
loading docks and layouts that don't

386
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:09,720
easily convert into anything else. One

387
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:11,960
mall owner spent $100 million over 3

388
00:13:09,720 --> 00:13:14,040
years just to subdivide a single old

389
00:13:11,960 --> 00:13:16,480
department store building into four

390
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:18,280
smaller stores. Multiply that cost and

391
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:20,560
that timeline across dozens of empty

392
00:13:18,280 --> 00:13:22,240
anchor boxes at once, which is exactly

393
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:24,600
what happened when Sears began closing

394
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:26,200
hundreds of stores simultaneously, and

395
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:28,000
you start to understand why analysts

396
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:29,880
describe the bankruptcy as something

397
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,760
that would cause pain for mall owners

398
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:34,280
for years afterward, not months. This is

399
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:35,960
the structural truth underneath the

400
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:37,760
whole industry.

401
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:39,800
A mall doesn't fail because business is

402
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:41,520
bad everywhere. It fails because one

403
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:43,400
piece falls out and the rest of the

404
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:45,120
structure was quietly built to depend on

405
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:46,640
that piece staying in place. To

406
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:48,880
understand how this plays out in the

407
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:51,320
real world, it helps to look at one real

408
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:54,120
mall from opening day to demolition.

409
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:56,960
Rolling Acres Mall opened in Akron, Ohio

410
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:59,040
in August of 1975 with Sears as its

411
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:01,120
first anchor and about 20 other stores

412
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:03,720
around it. Within a few years, it had

413
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:05,400
grown to over 140 stores with five

414
00:14:03,720 --> 00:14:07,560
different department store anchors, a

415
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:09,600
movie theater, and a food court. By the

416
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:11,440
mid-1990s, it was one of the busiest

417
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:13,200
shopping destinations in the region and

418
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:15,320
at the time a new Target store was

419
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:18,720
added, the mall was reported to be about

420
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:21,120
98% occupied. Then, slowly, the anchors

421
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:22,960
started leaving. A newer, renovated

422
00:14:21,120 --> 00:14:25,480
competing mall opened nearby in the late

423
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:27,440
'90s and pulled shoppers away. Target

424
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:29,360
relocated to a different town. Dillard's

425
00:14:27,440 --> 00:14:31,360
closed. One of the remaining department

426
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:33,120
stores was rebranded twice within 2

427
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:35,880
years before finally shutting down due

428
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:37,320
to poor sales. By 2006,

429
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:40,040
the mall's occupancy had fallen

430
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:41,320
dramatically and it was sold for $1.6

431
00:14:40,040 --> 00:14:43,080
million,

432
00:14:41,320 --> 00:14:45,080
a building that had once been valued at

433
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:46,880
well over $30 million.

434
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:49,160
The interior of the mall officially

435
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:50,640
closed in 2008, though two remaining

436
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:52,520
anchor stores stayed open through

437
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:54,960
separate outside entrances for a few

438
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:57,560
more years. Sears finally closed in

439
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:59,920
2011. The last remaining tenant, a

440
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:02,800
JCPenney outlet, closed at the very end

441
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:05,400
of 2013 and from that point the building

442
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:07,160
sat completely empty. What happened next

443
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:09,080
is the part that makes this story worth

444
00:15:07,160 --> 00:15:10,920
telling on a finance channel rather than

445
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:13,080
a ghost story channel. The empty

446
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:14,800
building attracted scrappers who tore

447
00:15:13,080 --> 00:15:17,600
out copper wiring and anything else of

448
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:19,400
value. In one incident, a man died after

449
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:21,240
being electrocuted while trying to steal

450
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:22,320
copper from a power box outside the

451
00:15:21,240 --> 00:15:24,320
mall.

452
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:25,920
Vandals broke skylights, which let rain

453
00:15:24,320 --> 00:15:27,960
and snow pour directly into the building

454
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:29,880
for years, rotting the floors and

455
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:31,800
ceilings from the inside. The city of

456
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:33,680
Akron eventually had to take ownership

457
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:35,680
of the property itself because the

458
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:37,840
owners had stopped paying property taxes

459
00:15:35,680 --> 00:15:39,760
altogether, owing more than a million

460
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:41,720
dollars in unpaid tax by the time the

461
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:43,400
city stepped in. The building was

462
00:15:41,720 --> 00:15:46,640
finally demolished in stages between

463
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:49,000
2016 and 2019. Amazon later built a

464
00:15:46,640 --> 00:15:50,520
distribution center on the same land.

465
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,880
Keep this in mind if you ever think a

466
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:55,920
full mall means a safe mall. Rolling

467
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:57,560
Acres was 98% occupied at its peak. It

468
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:59,240
took less than two decades to go from

469
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:01,240
that number to a pile of rubble and a

470
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:03,640
warehouse. The line between those two

471
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:05,520
states was not one dramatic event. It

472
00:16:03,640 --> 00:16:07,560
was a sequence of smaller ones, each one

473
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:09,520
making the next one more likely. The

474
00:16:07,560 --> 00:16:11,440
Sears bankruptcy and the slow collapse

475
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:13,040
of malls like Rolling Acres didn't

476
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:14,720
happen in a vacuum.

477
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:16,560
They happened at the same time online

478
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:19,120
shopping was taking over an increasing

479
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:21,240
share of retail spending in the country.

480
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:23,200
For most of the 20th century, a mall's

481
00:16:21,240 --> 00:16:25,600
core product wasn't really the stores

482
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:27,000
inside it. It was convenience. If you

483
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:29,160
wanted to compare five different brands

484
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,960
of shoes in one afternoon, the mall was

485
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:33,080
the only place in town that let you do

486
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:34,800
that in one trip. That convenience was

487
00:16:33,080 --> 00:16:37,440
the entire reason people would drive

488
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:39,560
across town and pay for parking. Online

489
00:16:37,440 --> 00:16:41,280
shopping broke that monopoly completely.

490
00:16:39,560 --> 00:16:42,960
A shopper can now compare hundreds of

491
00:16:41,280 --> 00:16:44,920
options from their couch, have it

492
00:16:42,960 --> 00:16:46,960
delivered in a day or two, and never

493
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:48,760
deal with parking, walking, or a

494
00:16:46,960 --> 00:16:50,600
checkout line at all. The mall's

495
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:52,760
original reason for existing,

496
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:54,920
convenience, simply stopped being unique

497
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:56,600
to malls. This is why the malls that

498
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,680
struggled most were often the one stuck

499
00:16:56,600 --> 00:17:00,240
in the middle. Not upscale enough to

500
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:02,440
sell an experience people couldn't get

501
00:17:00,240 --> 00:17:04,520
online, but not cheap enough to compete

502
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:07,079
purely on price with discount retailers,

503
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:08,839
either. The weakest malls, usually the

504
00:17:07,079 --> 00:17:10,439
ones already carrying financial strain

505
00:17:08,839 --> 00:17:12,560
or located in areas with declining

506
00:17:10,439 --> 00:17:15,000
populations, were hit first and hit

507
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:16,680
hardest, exactly the pattern analysts

508
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,680
described when Sears began closing

509
00:17:16,680 --> 00:17:20,560
stores. The strong malls getting

510
00:17:18,680 --> 00:17:22,760
stronger and the weak malls getting

511
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:24,680
weaker. At the same time, the mall

512
00:17:22,760 --> 00:17:26,000
owners who kept their properties alive

513
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:27,959
did it by changing what they were

514
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:30,160
selling. Instead of leasing space to

515
00:17:27,959 --> 00:17:31,640
more clothing retailers, many mall

516
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:33,960
owners started filling their old anchor

517
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:35,720
boxes and empty storefronts with things

518
00:17:33,960 --> 00:17:37,760
a computer screen genuinely cannot

519
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:39,800
replace. Restaurants that require a

520
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:41,840
physical table, gyms and fitness

521
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:43,880
studios, medical clinics and urgent care

522
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:46,280
centers, which need a physical location

523
00:17:43,880 --> 00:17:47,640
by definition, co-working office space,

524
00:17:46,280 --> 00:17:49,400
entertainment venues like movie

525
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:51,840
theaters, arcades, and indoor

526
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:54,040
attractions. Some malls converted entire

527
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:56,080
wings into apartments or hotels, turning

528
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:57,640
what used to be pure retail space into a

529
00:17:56,080 --> 00:17:59,160
mixed-use property with several

530
00:17:57,640 --> 00:18:01,920
different income streams instead of just

531
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:04,280
one. The underlying idea is simple, even

532
00:18:01,920 --> 00:18:06,480
if executing it is expensive and slow. A

533
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:08,440
mall used to sell products. The malls

534
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:10,600
that survived started selling reasons to

535
00:18:08,440 --> 00:18:12,280
physically leave the house. Reasons that

536
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:14,520
online shopping cannot deliver to your

537
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:16,840
door. So, does owning a shopping mall

538
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:19,240
actually work as a business? The honest

539
00:18:16,840 --> 00:18:20,800
answer depends entirely on which mall,

540
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:22,760
and the gap between the good outcome and

541
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:24,520
the bad outcome is enormous. In the

542
00:18:22,760 --> 00:18:26,560
optimistic scenario, you own a

543
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:28,160
well-located mall with strong anchor

544
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:30,200
tenants that still draw heavy foot

